# gitleaks findings accepted after triage — 2026-09-16.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. gitleaks-action@v2 picks its scope from the trigger:
# push and pull_request scan only that event's commits, schedule and
# workflow_dispatch scan the whole history. So `secret-scan` was green on
# every PR and red on every scheduled run, and the scheduled run was the one
# telling the truth. A permanently red check cannot distinguish a new finding
# from a standing one — the failure mode this repo already hit with the SCA
# job, where a real advisory would have landed invisibly.
#
# Note that docs/SECRETS_AUDIT.md (2026-07-30) concluded "no leaks found" in
# history. That conclusion does not hold: it scanned a bounded window of
# recent commits, and the findings below predate it.
#
# EVERY entry was triaged individually before being listed, and suppression
# came LAST — after each credential was confirmed to grant nothing. Had one
# still been live, the fix would have been revocation and this file would not
# exist. A baseline written first hides a working credential behind a green
# check. Keep that order if this file is ever extended.
#
# Operational detail about the historical credentials is deliberately not
# repeated here; this is a public repository, and the fingerprints below are
# already sufficient to re-run the triage.
#
